.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help \-H
This "help" flag cause the program to print out a description
of its operation and then exit immediately.
.TP
.B \-\-grid\-file=grdfile \-Igrdfile
Sets a GMT grid file to be read and displayed at startup.
This option is usually used only when \fBMBgrdviz\fP is started
automatically from some other process.
.TP
.B \-\-overlay\-file=grdfile \-Jgrdfile
Sets a GMT grid file to be read and displayed as an overlay at startup.
This option has no effect unless the \fB\-I\fP option is also used to
specify a primary grid file.
.TP
.B \-\-test \-T
Sets a test grid to be calculated and displayed at startup.This
allows the program to be tested even when a grid file is
unavailable.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose \-V
Normally, \fBMBgrdviz\fP outputs limited information to the stderr stream.
If the \fB\-V\fP flag is given, then \fBMBgrdviz\fP works in a "verbose" mode and
outputs the program version being used, all error status messages,
and a large amount of other information including all of the
beams flagged or zeroed.

.SH INTERACTIVE CONTROLS: MBgrdviz Startup Window
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Open Primary Grid>
This menu button pops up a file browser that allows the user to
select an input \fBGMT\fP grid file. If the OK button is clicked, \fBMBgrdviz\fP will
attempt to read a topography grid from the specified file. If successful,
a new \fBMBview\fP window will appear displaying the topography. This item
is active until all ten \fBMBview\fP windows are in use.
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Open Site>
This menu button pops up a file browser that allows the user to
select an input site file (see the site file format below). A site file
specifies one or more individual locations that can be displayed and
interactively moved. Sites are shared by all \fBMBview\fP windows. This
item, like the other file items below, is active once an \fBMBview\fP window
is open, and acts on the first \fBMBview\fP window; the <Action> menu of each
\fBMBview\fP window has the same items acting on that window.
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Open Route>
This menu button pops up a file browser that allows the user to
select an input route file (see the route file format below).
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Open Navigation>
This menu button pops up a file browser that allows the user to select an
\fBMB-System\fP datalist of data files whose navigation is to be displayed
(see <Action->Open Navigation>).
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Open Swath>
This menu button pops up a file browser that allows the user to select an
\fBMB-System\fP datalist of swath data files whose navigation and swath
coverage are to be displayed (see <Action->Open Swath Data>).
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Open Vector>
This menu button pops up a file browser that allows the user to select a
vector file (see the vector file format below).
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Open Polyline>
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Open Polygon>
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Open Point>
These menu buttons pop up a file browser that allows the user to select an
MB-System polyline (".pln"), polygon (".plg"), or point (".pts") file to be
read and displayed in the first \fBMBview\fP window, as with the
corresponding items of the \fBMBview\fP <Action> menu.
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Save Site>
This menu button pops up a file browser that allows the user to specify an
output site file to which all sites are written. It is active when sites exist.
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Save Route>
This menu button pops up a file browser that allows the user to specify an
output route file to which the selected routes, or all routes if none are
selected, are written. It is active when routes exist.
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<File->Quit>
This button causes the program to exit (mostly) gracefully.
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<Realtime->Setup>
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<Realtime->Start>
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<Realtime->Stop>
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<Realtime->Pause>
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<Realtime->Resume>
The \fBRealtime\fP menu and its setup dialog are intended for the display of
realtime navigation, which is not yet implemented; the menu is inactive.
.TP
.B [MBgrdviz]:<Help->About>
This button causes the program to bring up a dialog showing the
program's name, version, and authors.

.SH INTERACTIVE CONTROLS: MBview Main Window
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Map Display>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->3D Display>
These items select a 2D map view or a 3D perspective view. The map display is
not available with the spheroid display projection, which is always shown in
3D.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Topography>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Topography Slope>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Overlay>
These items select the grid that is colored: the primary topography, the
magnitude of the topographic slope, or the overlay grid (available once an
overlay grid has been read with <Action->Open Overlay Grid>). Each has its own
colortable, color bounds, histogram equalization, and shading settings, which
are restored when it is selected again.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Histogram Equalization>
This toggle colors the current grid using histogram equalization instead of
a linear stretch between the color bounds.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->No Shading>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Shading by Illumination>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Shading by Slope>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Shading by Overlay>
These items select the shading of the current grid: none, illumination by a
light source, the slope magnitude, or the values of the overlay grid
(available once an overlay grid has been read). The shading parameters are
set in the <Controls->Shading> dialog or with the <Mouse->Shading> mouse mode.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Topography Contours>
This toggle displays topographic contours at the interval set in the
<Controls->Colors & Contours> dialog.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Sites>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Routes>
These toggles display or hide the sites and routes. They are active once
site or route display is enabled. Hiding sites or routes also leaves the
corresponding editing mouse mode.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Navigation>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Navigation Swath Bounds>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Draped Navigation>
These toggles display the navigation tracks, the outlines of the swath
coverage, and the navigation draped on the topography. They are active once
navigation has been read.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Vector>
This toggle displays the vector data, shown as colored balls at each point;
it is active once vector data have been read.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Polylines>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Polygons>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Points>
These toggles display or hide the GIS polylines, polygons, and points in this
window. They are active once data of the type have been read or imported, and
are turned on when data are first read into the window.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Haxby Colortable>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Bright Rainbow Colortable>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Muted Rainbow Colortable>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Grayscale Colortable>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Flat Gray Colortable>
These items select the colortable for the current grid. The flat gray
colortable is a single shade of gray, useful with shading.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Sealevel1 Colortable>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Sealevel2 Colortable>
These colortables color values below zero (sea level) with the Haxby colors
and values above zero with land colors: pale yellow to brown for Sealevel1,
and pale yellow through browns to green for Sealevel2.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<View->Profile>
This item opens or closes the profile window (see the Profile window below).
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->Colors & Contours>
This item opens the Colors and Contours dialog (see below).
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->2D Controls>
This item opens the 2D Parameters dialog (see below).
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->3D Controls>
This item opens the 3D Parameters dialog (see below).
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->Shading>
This item opens the Shading Parameters dialog (see below).
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->Resolution>
This item opens the Rendering Resolution dialog (see below).
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->Projections>
This item opens the Projections dialog (see below).
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->Site List>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->Route List>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->Navigation List>
These items open the windows listing the sites, routes, and navigation files
(see below). The lists are shared by all \fBMBview\fP windows.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->Polyline List>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->Polygon List>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Controls->Point List>
These menu buttons bring up windows listing the GIS polylines, polygons, or
points (see the Polyline List, Polygon List, and Point List windows below).
Like <Controls->Navigation List>, they are always active, even before any
data of the type have been read or imported.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Mouse->Pan & Zoom>
This mouse mode (the default) uses the left button to pick, the middle
button to pan, and the right button to zoom (see MOUSE ACTIONS below).
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Mouse->Rotate Model>
This mouse mode, available in the 3D display, uses the middle button to
rotate the topography model and the right button to change the vertical
exaggeration.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Mouse->Rotate View>
This mouse mode, available in the 3D display, uses the middle button to
rotate the viewpoint and the right button to zoom.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Mouse->Shading>
This mouse mode uses the middle button to move the light source (for shading
by illumination) and the right button to change the shading magnitude.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Mouse->Pick Area>
This mouse mode uses the left button to define a region, the middle button
to define an area, and the right button to change the width of the area.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Mouse->Edit Sites>
This mouse mode, active once site display is enabled, is used to select,
move, add, and delete sites. It turns on the display of sites.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Mouse->Edit Routes>
This mouse mode, active once route display is enabled, is used to select
routes and to add, move, and delete waypoints. It turns on the display of
routes.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Mouse->Select Nav>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Mouse->Select Nav File>
These mouse modes, active once navigation has been read, select sections of
navigation or whole navigation files with the middle button and deselect them
with the right button.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->About MBview...>
This item brings up a dialog showing information about \fBMBview\fP.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Open Overlay Grid>
This item pops up a file browser that allows the user to select a \fBGMT\fP
grid to be displayed as an overlay on the topography, either colored in place
of the topography (<View->Overlay>) or used for shading
(<View->Shading by Overlay>).
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Open Site File>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Open Route File>
These items pop up a file browser that allows the user to select a site file
(".ste") or route file (".rte") to be read and displayed.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Open Navigation>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Open Swath Data>
These items pop up a file browser that allows the user to select an
\fBMB-System\fP datalist (".mb-1"). The navigation of each data file in the
datalist, within the bounds of the grid, is read and displayed as a track
named for the file; processed files are used where the datalist calls for
them, and ASCII xyz files are skipped. <Action->Open Navigation> reads the
".fnv" navigation files where they exist; <Action->Open Swath Data> reads
the ".fbt" bathymetry files where they exist and also gives the outline of
the swath coverage. The navigation is decimated according to the grid
spacing.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Open Vector File>
This item pops up a file browser that allows the user to select a vector file
(see the vector file format below), which is displayed as colored balls
along its tracks.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Open Polyline File>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Open Polygon File>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Open Point File>
These menu buttons pop up a file browser that allows the user to select an
MB-System polyline (".pln"), polygon (".plg"), or point (".pts") file. All of
the features in the file are read and displayed, whether or not they overlap
the grid, with each layer of the file becoming a layer in \fBMBgrdviz\fP.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Import Shapefile>
This menu button pops up a file browser that allows the user to select an ESRI
shapefile (".shp"). The polylines, polygons, or points of the shapefile that
overlap the grid displayed in this window are imported and displayed as a new
layer; the number imported and skipped is printed to the terminal.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Polyline File>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Polygon File>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Point File>
These menu buttons pop up a file browser that allows the user to specify an
output file to which all of the polylines, polygons, or points, whether shown
or hidden, are written in the MB-System format, keeping their layers, names,
colors, and sizes.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Export Polylines as Shapefile>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Export Polygons as Shapefile>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Export Points as Shapefile>
These menu buttons pop up a file browser that allows the user to specify an
output ESRI shapefile (".shp" is added if missing). All of the polylines,
polygons, or points are written in WGS84 longitude and latitude with NAME,
COLOR, and SIZE attributes (and FILLCOLOR for polygons); an existing shapefile
of the same name is replaced.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Site File>
This item pops up a file browser that allows the user to specify an output
site file to which all sites are written. It is active when sites exist.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Route File>
This item pops up a file browser that allows the user to specify an output
route file to which the selected routes, or all routes if none are selected,
are written. It is active when routes exist, as are the other route export
items below.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Route File Reversed>
This item writes the selected routes, or all routes, as a route file with the
points of each route in reverse order.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Risi Script File (variable heading)>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Risi Script File (static heading)>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Risi 2 Script File (variable heading)>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Risi 2 Script File (static heading)>
These items write the selected routes, or all routes, as mission scripts for
the Risi vehicle controller, with the waypoints given in meters relative to the
first waypoint. The Risi 2 scripts give each leg as a relative turn
("heading rel") and a move ("move bearing range, bearing", with the bearing
relative to the vehicle heading), assuming that the vehicle starts at the first
waypoint with heading 0. With variable heading the vehicle turns to face along
each leg; with static heading it turns once to the bearing of the first leg,
keeps that heading on all legs, and turns back to heading 0 at the end.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Route as Degrees + Decimal Minutes File>
This item writes the waypoints of all routes as latitude and longitude in
degrees and decimal minutes.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Route as Hypack LNW File>
This item writes all routes as a Hypack line (".lnw") file, with the
waypoints in UTM eastings and northings.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Route as Greensea YML File>
This item writes the selected route (exactly one route must be selected, or
exist) as a Greensea mission YAML file.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Route as TECDIS LST File>
This item writes the waypoints of all routes as a TECDIS route list file.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Route as Kongsberg DP waypoint File>
This item writes all routes as a Kongsberg dynamic positioning waypoint file.
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Route as SIS ASCIIPlan line File>
.TP
.B [MBview]:<Action->Save Route as SIS ASCIIPlan route File>
